# Netlify _redirects — paired with netlify.toml for fallback rules.
# Order: most specific first.

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Withdrawn resources — NDMA security assessment, Finding 2
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These paths held fabricated certificate-holder data (names,
# programmes, regions, grades, issue dates) and AI-generated portraits
# captioned with individuals' names. The files have been removed from
# the source tree and from the deployment artefact.
#
# These rules are belt-and-braces: a Netlify drop deploy was observed
# to keep serving files that had been removed from the upload, leaving
# them reachable as orphans. The trailing "!" forces the rule to apply
# even when a file still exists at the path, so the resource is
# unreachable regardless of what lingers in the deploy store.
#
# 410 Gone (not 404) is deliberate: it states the resource existed and
# has been permanently withdrawn, which is the accurate signal to a
# scanner and to any cached index.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

/data/certificates.json     /pages/404.html   410!
/js/stories-spotlight.js    /pages/404.html   410!
/media/images/grad-*        /pages/404.html   410!

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Structural redirects
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

/index-v2.html          /                              301
/pages/apply.html       /pages/contact.html            301
/apply                  /pages/contact.html            301

# 404 fallback to the dedicated 404 page (keeps the chrome consistent)
/*                      /pages/404.html                404
